Your night-fire hypothesis is plausible as a local amplifier, but I don’t think it is the main explanation. The first correction is that the Sun actually was central or even supreme in several major traditions: Ra/Re was one of ancient Egypt’s highest gods and was tied to kingship; Akhenaten briefly elevated the solar Aten above the traditional pantheon; Surya was once ranked among Hinduism’s major deities; and Inti was a chief Inca deity. 

What is interesting is why that did not happen everywhere. I think several deeper mechanisms stack together:

* Divine rank was not an astronomy ranking. Ancient people were not choosing the “largest celestial body” as CEO. Zeus/Jupiter’s supremacy came from the inherited Sky Father role—sovereignty, daylight sky, thunder, oath, political order—not because anyone knew the planet Jupiter was physically enormous. Comparative linguistic evidence traces Zeus and related figures back toward an Indo-European Sky Father tradition. 
* Uncertainty attracts ritual attention. The Sun is spectacular but extremely reliable: it rises tomorrow. Rain, drought, lightning, storms, war, fertility, disease, and harvest failure are much less predictable. Anthropology has repeatedly found stronger ritualization around domains where consequential outcomes are uncertain; cross-cultural research also links resource and weather uncertainty with beliefs about gods controlling weather.  That could help explain why storm/rain gods repeatedly acquire enormous political and religious weight.
* Pantheons often mirror human political structures. The chief god can effectively become the divine king. Mesopotamia gives an unusually clean example: as Babylon gained political power, its patron Marduk rose from relative obscurity to head of the pantheon. The Met explicitly notes that a city’s political strength could be reflected in its deity’s rank.  So “chief god” may tell you as much about who ruled the humans as about what dominated the sky.
* Different celestial objects naturally acquire different narrative jobs. Shamash/Utu, for example, became associated with justice partly because the Sun metaphorically sees everything.  The Sun can therefore become judge, life-giver, king, eye, rebirth mechanism, or clock without necessarily occupying the same narrative niche as the unpredictable thunder-king.
* Ecology matters. Egypt’s intense, reliable sunlight makes solar theology almost obvious. In agricultural regions where unpredictable rain determines survival, a rain/storm deity has another kind of leverage. That should not be turned into a universal rule, but it gives us a useful environmental variable to test.

And I do like your night-storytelling idea as an additional mechanism. Imagine the actual perceptual field of oral culture at night: dozens of stars, wandering planets, constellations, the Moon changing shape, meteors, the Milky Way—and fire in the foreground. That environment supplies an enormous cast of visible characters. The Sun, meanwhile, is absent exactly while many stories may be circulating.

But I would treat that as a hypothesis rather than a historical conclusion. Religious narratives were also enacted in daylight ceremonies, sunrise rites, agricultural routines, architecture, sacrifice, royal ideology, and seasonal festivals—not just campfire storytelling. So nighttime visibility probably cannot explain the whole pattern.

There’s also a beautiful inversion hiding here: perhaps the Sun sometimes has less narrative freedom precisely because it is too dominant.

The Sun does essentially one overwhelming thing with extraordinary regularity:

appears → crosses sky → disappears → returns.

That makes it superb for cosmology, death/rebirth, kingship, time, order, and inevitability—but comparatively poor as a capricious character. Storms can arrive, refuse to arrive, destroy a village, save a crop, answer a prayer, or appear angry. They behave more like agents.

In Pantheon terms, that suggests another primitive:

Phenomenal magnitude ≠ narrative agency.

A thing can dominate the physical field while something smaller dominates the causal, emotional, political, or uncertainty field.

And that may be why Jupiter/Zeus can sit on the throne while Helios drives across the background: the brightest object is not necessarily the object humans experience as making decisions.

That one generalizes far beyond religion.